---
import ProjectLayout from "../layouts/project-layout.astro";
import Button1 from "../components/button1.astro";
import Image from "../components/image.astro";

import handLandmarks from "../assets/projects/page-images/handLandmarks.png"
import camDemo from "../assets/projects/page-images/cmrmindemo.gif"
---

<ProjectLayout name="the cameremin" colour="#75ba75">
    <p>The Cameremin was a project I developed with Tyler Kettner to combine machine
        learning with music. The intention behind the project was to create an intuitive 
        instrument that could be used on any computer as a proof of concept of what machine learning
        algorithms can do. It combines the OpenCV python plugin with Max/MSP to convert hand positioning
        into sound.
    </p>
    
    <p>
        The python code implements OpenCV to start a video feed using a webcam or external camera.
        Then it uses MediaPipe to scan each photo in the video feed for hands. Its tuned to track two hands. 
        OpenCV can then draw points on the frames depending on the finger location MediaPipe predicts.
        Points are adjusted to highlight the index fingers and thumbs, which are the main control 
        features for the instrument.
    </p>
    <Image src={handLandmarks.src} colour="#75ba75" class="landmarks"></Image>
    <p>
        For the right hand, the position of the thumb is described in 2D space, via the resolution
        of the camera, and the Y value is encoded into OSC and sent over a UDP localhost socket server
        to be recieved by the Max patch. This is then scaled and routed to describe the pitch of the instrument.
        Similarily, all other values desired from the hands are encoded and sent over UDP to Max.
    </p>

    <p>
        The distance between the index and thumb for both hands are drawn and calculated using a simple
        distance equation. The value of the left hand is used to adjust the volume, where a larger distance
        between fingers is a quieter sound, and a smaller distance is louder. Although the decisions may feel
        less intuitive, the reverse is particularily harder to create responsive dynamics when playing the instrument.
    </p>
    <Image src={camDemo.src} colour="#75ba75", class="camDemo"></Image>
    <p>
        In the right hand, the distance between the index and thumb is used to control the vibrato of the instrument where
        an increase of distance increases the vibrato, and a decrease does the reverse.
        
    </p>
    <p>For a more detailed analysis on the creation of each part, the paper on this project, or the 
        code contained in the repo, are below. 
    </p>
    <span></span>
    <div class="btn">
        <Button1 href="https://github.com/AndrewKehler/cameremin/blob/main/The%20Cameremin(Camera%20Theremin).pdf">See Paper</Button1>
        <Button1 href="https://github.com/AndrewKehler/cameremin">See Project</Button1>
    </div>
    
</ProjectLayout>
<style>
	@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Graduate&family=Intel+One+Mono:ital,wght@0,300..700;1,300..700&display=swap');

	.graduate-regular {
		font-family: "Graduate", serif;
		font-weight: 400;
		font-style: normal;
	}
	body{
		margin: 0;
		overflow-x: hidden;
		
	}

</style>
<style>
    .btn{
        display: flex;
        gap: 5%;
        align-self: center;
        justify-self: center;
        justify-content: center;
        width: 100%;
        font-size: clamp(.5em, 0.8vw, 2em);
    }

    .camDemo{
        position: relative;
        width: 25%;
        justify-self: flex-start;
        margin: -1% 0 0 -8%;
        padding: 7% 0 4% 0;
        /* transform: scaleX(-1); */
    }
    .camDemo :global(img){
        width: 85%;
        margin: 0% 0% -10% 8%;
        border-radius: 20%;
        /* transform: scaleX(-1); */
    }
    .landmarks{
        position: relative;
        width: 25%;
        justify-self: flex-end;
        margin: -3% -8% 0 0;
        transform: scaleX(-1);
    }
    .landmarks :global(img){
        transform: scaleX(-1);
    }
</style>